  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.12415v1 [cs.CY] 15 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Prompt Commons: Collective Prompting as Governance for Urban AI</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rashid Mushkani
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Université de Montréal
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Mila – Quebec AI Institute
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) are entering urban governance, yet their outputs are highly sensitive to prompts that carry value judgments. We propose <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prompt Commons</em>—a versioned, community-maintained repository of prompts with governance metadata, licensing, and moderation—to steer model behaviour toward pluralism. Using a Montréal dataset (443 human prompts; 3,317 after augmentation), we pilot three governance states (<em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open</em>, <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">curated</em>, <em id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">veto-enabled</em>). On a contested policy benchmark, a single-author prompt yields <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> neutral outcomes; commons-governed prompts raise neutrality to <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}52\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>48</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>52</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}52\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> while retaining decisiveness where appropriate. In a synthetic incident log, a veto-enabled regime reduces time-to-remediation for harmful outputs from <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.5\pm\pm 8.9\text{\,}\mathrm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>30.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>8.9</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.5\pm\pm 8.9\text{\,}\mathrm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (open) to <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.6\pm\pm 1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.6</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.6\pm\pm 1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. We outline licensing (CC BY/BY–SA for prompts with optional OpenRAIL-style restrictions for artefacts), auditable moderation, and safeguards against dominance capture. Prompt governance offers a practical lever for cities to align AI with local values and accountability.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S0.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Keywords:</h4>

<div id="S0.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">prompt engineering, governance, urban AI, community moderation, licensing, pluralism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Urban planners increasingly consult generative AI systems for drafting, summarising, coding, and producing imagery to support community engagement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani et al., 2025e</a>)</cite>.
Unlike traditional datasets or models, LLMs can change behaviour markedly with small prompt variations: a single phrase can alter tone, policy stance, and inclusivity.
Prior work has emphasised downstream controls—documentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebru et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, dataset governance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebru et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and post-training alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>—but the act of <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompting</em> has rarely been treated as a first-class governance object.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent urban AI scholarship indicates that cities are already experimenting with LLMs in planning, management, and policy settings, while warning against deployments that neglect urban complexity. A systematic review maps the breadth of applications and recurring gaps in rigor and evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Strategic informatics work synthesised from Shenzhen’s policy practice proposes cross-cutting plans spanning technology systems, application scenarios, literacy, and governance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu and Liu, 2025</a>)</cite>. Interviews with city managers in Australia and the US report pragmatic constraints—organizational preparedness, data, and talent—that mediate adoption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yigitcanlar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These perspectives extend earlier arguments that <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local</em> innovation and governance capacity are decisive for responsible AI in cities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allam and Dhunny, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirwan and Fu, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate whether human-authored prompts, when deployed as individual artefacts, reduce neutrality (i.e., increase decisiveness toward particular value claims). Decisiveness is not inherently undesirable; cities often need concrete recommendations. The concern arises when prompt authorship is opaque and individualised, which risks encoding one group’s values as system defaults.
We therefore propose <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prompt Commons</em>: a civic infrastructure where communities author, review, license, and version prompts collectively, drawing on commons governance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ostrom, 1990</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlisle and Gruby, 2019</a>)</cite> and open-source practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Mahony and Ferraro, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">The Linux Foundation, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> We (i) introduce a versioned repository and governance metadata for prompts; (ii) evaluate behavioural shifts across governance states using a pilot benchmark of contested urban choices; and (iii) specify IP/licensing and moderation rules designed to protect community authors while enabling reuse.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Documentation and transparency.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model Cards and Datasheets set expectations and report performance across subgroups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebru et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Prompt Commons complements these by documenting <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inputs</em> that steer model behaviour, not only outputs. In the urban domain, recent syntheses emphasise surfacing context, value trade-offs, and domain limitations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Treating prompts as first-class, versioned objects extends transparency to the moment of interaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Post-training governance and alignment.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RLHF and instruct-tuning steer models to human preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; Constitutional AI aligns behaviour to a principle set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These approaches may reflect the biases of those defining preferences or principles. Democratic or dynamic value alignment aims to involve diverse stakeholders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, with social-choice–informed aggregation highlighting how rules affect outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Prompt governance is complementary: instead of changing model weights or selecting a single constitution, Prompt Commons enables communities to <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">configure</em> behaviour at use time in a visible, auditable way <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Urban AI and LLM applications.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reviews and case studies note growing use of LLMs in urban analytics and decision-making alongside methodological and governance concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu and Liu, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yigitcanlar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These extend arguments that cities are central to responsible AI deployment and must innovate in governance to align capabilities with local needs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allam and Dhunny, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirwan and Fu, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani et al., 2025f</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani and Koseki, 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt design, sensitivity, neutrality.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt components (instructions, role, context, output formats, examples) and structured tactics for planning tasks have been synthesised <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Foundational work showed that large models can adapt to new tasks with few examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, but such flexibility brings high sensitivity to wording; reasoning prompts can improve factual correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite> while also increasing confident but wrong answers in some settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajani et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. In normative urban queries, small wording changes can tilt value stances; Prompt Commons responds by distributing authorship and making value claims explicit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bias, ideology, and neutrality in LLMs.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evidence shows that LLMs and alignment artefacts can display systematic political leanings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fulay et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our pilot reflects this sensitivity: a single strong prompt yields fewer neutral choices than governed commons prompts. Prior findings on multi-perspective prompting and aggregation suggest that ensembles can moderate extremes; we operationalise this through governance states (open/curated/veto-enabled) and a weighted-ensemble method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Commons and community moderation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ostrom’s principles for governing shared resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ostrom, 1990</a>)</cite> have inspired digital-commons adaptations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mozilla Foundation, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dulong de Rosnay and Stalder, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bollier, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scott, 2018</a>)</cite>. Open-source communities blend meritocracy with democratic mechanisms as governance formalises <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Mahony and Ferraro, 2007</a>)</cite>, a pattern we echo. Volunteer moderation is critical but labour-intensive; studies highlight the need for clear rules, public logs, and appeal processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tabassum et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weld et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We adopt these lessons—e.g., public issues, auditable takedowns, <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt strikes</em>—to keep the Commons accountable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Security and harm.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Embedding LLMs in civic workflows introduces attack surfaces from prompt injection to insecure output handling. Formal analyses and surveys motivate operational metrics like <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">time-to-remediation</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Community-responsive governance (including minority veto) can be a practical control, alongside guidance such as the OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">OWASP GenAI Security Project, 2023</a>)</cite>. We also engage critical perspectives that caution against over-reliance on ever-larger models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender et al., 2021</a>)</cite> by treating the model as a fallible component within a socio-technical process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Data and Participants</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Community prompts from Montréal</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We convened community partners through local civil-society organisations across disability advocacy, immigrant support, seniors’ services, women’s groups, LGBTQ+ organisations, and neighbourhood associations; plus urban practitioners and a national cultural institution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Participants authored <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">443</span> prompts describing urban scenes and values (e.g., accessibility, biodiversity, transit, safety) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani and Koseki, 2025a</a>)</cite>.
We augmented these to <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3,317</span> prompts via de-duplication, paraphrasing, and scenario expansion.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Papers describing the dataset and methodological details are omitted for the peer review process.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Descriptive statistics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human-authored prompts have mean length <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">22.6</span> words (median 19), compared to <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">31.7</span> words after augmentation.
Vocabulary entropy rises from <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7.53</span> (human) to <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8.39</span> bits (augmented), indicating higher lexical diversity.
Top tokens across the augmented set are <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">street</em> (1,506), <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">park</em> (1,138), <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trees</em> (906), <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">benches</em> (732), and <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">water</em> (702).
Equity-related tokens appear with non-trivial frequency: <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wheelchair</em> (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.6\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.6\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> of prompts), <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">metro</em> (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.3\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.3\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">biodiversity</em> (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.7\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.7</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.7\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>); <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">LGBTQ+</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Indigenous</em> appear less often (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}1.4\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>1.4</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}1.4\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), highlighting inclusion gaps for targeted recruitment going forward.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12415v1/0_self_declared_participants.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/285;" width="476" height="285" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Self-declared participant categories by age group (pilot).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12415v1/0_word-cloud-prompts.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/321;" width="476" height="321" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Prompt topics word cloud (human-authored set).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Prompt Commons: Design and Governance</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Versioned repository and metadata</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The repository maintains prompts as plain text with the following metadata:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author group(s)</span> (e.g., seniors, disability advocacy, LGBTQ+, ethnic or religious minority).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Locale</span> (e.g., Montréal neighbourhood, street name).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intended value claim</span> (e.g., safety, accessibility, biodiversity, conviviality).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Change log</span> with semantic versioning for prompts and collections.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each change is an issue or pull request; maintainers triage and merge with an auditable log (who, when, rationale).
Beyond the checklist below, maintainers keep a running <em id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">curation memo</em> that describes how inclusions and exclusions relate to quotas and local policy instruments. This memo, together with issue discussions, provides the interpretive glue that links individual community contributions to a legible institutional memory about the territory where prompts will be deployed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Governance states we study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare three governance states:</p>
<dl id="S4.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S4.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">any authenticated contributor; light moderation (spam and safety only).</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Curated:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">maintainers apply quotas for inclusion (per author group and topic), require metadata completeness, and merge only prompts that pass a public checklist (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Public checklist used in curation ‣ Prompt Commons: Collective Prompting as Governance for Urban AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Veto-enabled:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">as curated, plus a <em id="S4.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minority veto</em>—representative organisations can flag outputs they deem harmful; flagged prompts are quarantined pending review with an appeal process.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This design borrows from commons governance and moderation practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ostrom, 1990</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Mahony and Ferraro, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weld et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. It also reflects polycentricity: neighbourhood-level prompt sets and organisations can operate semi-autonomously and federate into a city layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlisle and Gruby, 2019</a>)</cite>. In practice, nested repositories and rotating maintainer seats can enact these ideas with lightweight, auditable processes, mirroring open-source governance while ensuring that affected groups have voice and visibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Licensing and IP</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompts are textual creative works; a permissive licence maximises reuse while protecting attribution. We recommend:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompts</span>: Creative Commons CC BY 4.0 or BY–SA 4.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Creative Commons, 2013b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Creative Commons, 2013a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Creative Commons, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Derived model artefacts</span>: OpenRAIL-style licences where use restrictions (e.g., no harassment, no biometric surveillance) are desired <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hugging Face, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">RAIL Initiative, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keller and Bonato, 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keller and Bonato, 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BigCode Project, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">ifrOSS, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Responsible-AI licensing is evolving; empirical analyses document the spread of use-restricted licences and argue for “standardised customisation” so behavioural clauses remain comprehensible yet adaptable to domain needs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDuff et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Contractor et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. For public-sector and civic deployments, such legal instruments complement governance-by-process: communities retain attribution and set guardrails for derivative artefacts without foreclosing legitimate reuse.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Research questions</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> How does a community-maintained Prompt Commons shift model behaviour across neutrality/decisiveness and subgroup satisfaction? 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> Which licensing and moderation rules protect community authors while enabling reuse?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Protocol</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We create five evaluation methods that operationalise the governance states above:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M0 Single author</span> (baseline): a strong, opinionated prompt by one individual.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M1 Open Commons</span>: random prompt from the open set.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M2 Curated Commons</span>: prompt selected from the curated set satisfying quotas.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M3 Veto-enabled</span>: curated set with minority-veto log enabled.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M4 Weighted ensemble</span>: sample <math id="S5.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> prompts across groups and summarise with an aggregator instruction (<em id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“deliberate and propose a compromise”</em>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on a contested-choice benchmark (transport, public space, and streetscape trade-offs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>.
The model must recommend one of three mutually exclusive options: <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left-leaning</em>, <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right-leaning</em>, or <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Neutral/compromise</em> (labels for the benchmark classes).
We report proportions per method (Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Protocol ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Prompt Commons: Collective Prompting as Governance for Urban AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), a decisiveness score <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=1-\text{Neutral}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>Neutral</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=1-\text{Neutral}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a subgroup satisfaction score using a 7-point Likert scale gathered from community reviewers.
For operational safety we also track <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">time-to-remediation</em> for harmful outputs flagged through the issue tracker.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12415v1/prompt-performance.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/305;" width="476" height="305" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Outcome proportions by method (pilot). Single-author prompting reduces neutrality in our setup; curated/veto-enabled governance increases neutrality and reduces dispersion across groups.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Results</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Neutrality and decisiveness.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The single-author baseline (M0) yields <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> Left, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> Right, and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> Neutral (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D{=}0.76" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.76</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D{=}0.76</annotation></semantics></math>).
Open and curated commons (M1–M3) yield <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}52\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>48</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>52</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}52\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> Neutral and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}26\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>24</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>26</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}26\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> for Left and Right categories (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in[0.48,0.52]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.48</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.52</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in[0.48,0.52]</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating rebalancing toward more cautious recommendations in this benchmark.
Weighted ensembling (M4) sits in between (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D{=}0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D{=}0.49</annotation></semantics></math>).
One interpretation, in light of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajani et al. (2019)</a></cite>, is that community prompts add deliberative context that increases <em id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">productive hesitation</em> on ambiguous problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Subgroup satisfaction.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across six self-identified groups (seniors, women, ethnic minority, disability, LGBTQ+, religious minority), average satisfaction rises from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.35\pm 0.86" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.35</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.86</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.35\pm 0.86</annotation></semantics></math> (M0) to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.92\pm 0.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.92</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.44</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.92\pm 0.44</annotation></semantics></math> (M2) and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.48\pm 0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.48</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.48\pm 0.66</annotation></semantics></math> (M3) on a 7-point scale.
Dispersion across groups (Gini over group means) shrinks from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.096</annotation></semantics></math> (M0) to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.043" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.043</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.043</annotation></semantics></math> (M2), suggesting that curation reduces disparity in perceived fit. Aggregating multiple perspectives can moderate ideological biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fulay et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Moderation efficiency.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We stress-tested governance states via a synthetic incident log of 50 flagged items per state.
Mean time-to-remediation improves from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30.5\pm\pm 8.9\text{\,}\mathrm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>30.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>8.9</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30.5\pm\pm 8.9\text{\,}\mathrm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (open) to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.8\pm\pm 3.2\text{\,}\mathrm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>11.8</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>3.2</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.8\pm\pm 3.2\text{\,}\mathrm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (curated) and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.6\pm\pm 1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.6</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.6\pm\pm 1.5\text{\,}\mathrm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (veto-enabled).
This aligns with security guidance calling for robust oversight and rapid incident response in LLM deployments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">OWASP GenAI Security Project, 2023</a>)</cite> and with moderation research showing that timely, well-supported volunteer action reduces harm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tabassum et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Interpretation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Results support the hypothesis that <em id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">individual prompting can reduce neutrality</em>, while collective governance recentres pluralism and operational safety in our setting.
In contexts where decisiveness is needed (e.g., emergency response), M0-like behaviour may be desirable; for long-horizon city-making, the commons-based regimes (M2–M3) appear preferable.
Seen through a social-choice lens, Prompt Commons acts as a light-touch mechanism for aggregating heterogeneous values at use time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani et al., 2025d</a>)</cite>, complementing post-training alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>A governance and licensing recipe for cities</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Rules of the Prompt Commons (Ostrom-informed)</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We translate Ostrom’s principles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ostrom, 1990</a>)</cite> to prompt governance:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Boundaries:</span> define admissible locales and stakeholder groups; require affiliation declaration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local fit:</span> require a locale tag; maintainers prioritise prompts grounded in local policy instruments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Collective choice:</span> public proposals and votes for quota changes and checklists (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Public checklist used in curation ‣ Prompt Commons: Collective Prompting as Governance for Urban AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Monitoring:</span> public audit log; every merged prompt carries an issue link.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graduated sanctions:</span> from soft fixes to temporary suspensions; logs remain public.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conflict resolution:</span> fast lanes for appeal (72-hour target SLA).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recognition of rights:</span> municipal partners recognise the Commons as an advisory asset.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">8.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nested enterprises:</span> multiple neighbourhood-level commons feeding a citywide layer (<em id="S6.I1.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polycentricity</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlisle and Gruby, 2019</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These rules are not abstract checkboxes. Digital-commons practice shows that translating boundaries, monitoring, and sanctions into visible workflows (issues, pull requests, labels, takedown logs) builds legitimacy and resilience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’Mahony and Ferraro, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bollier, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scott, 2018</a>)</cite>. For Prompt Commons, we bind these mechanics to municipal decision rhythms: locale tags reference policy instruments; quota debates happen before budgeting cycles; and vetoes trigger time-boxed remediation with public rationales that future maintainers can audit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Licensing choices</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Default.</span> CC BY 4.0 for all prompts (attribution required; commercial use permitted) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Creative Commons, 2013a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Creative Commons, 2013b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Creative Commons, 2025</a>)</cite>. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Share-alike option.</span> CC BY–SA 4.0 if communities want derivatives to remain open. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Use-restricted artefacts.</span> When the Commons distributes models, consider OpenRAIL-style licences to prohibit specific harms (e.g., stalking, biometric surveillance) while remaining open for legitimate uses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hugging Face, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">RAIL Initiative, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keller and Bonato, 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Keller and Bonato, 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BigCode Project, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">ifrOSS, 2024</a>)</cite>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Recent analyses recommend <em id="S6.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">standardised customisation</em> for use restrictions to reduce confusion while enabling contextual guardrails (e.g., for health or public-sector deployments) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDuff et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Contractor et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Our stance: clear CC licences for prompts; modular, community-negotiated restrictions for derived artefacts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Moderation &amp; “prompt strikes”</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Moderation combines (i) automated checks (toxicity, Personally Identifiable Information (PII)), (ii) maintainer review, and (iii) community veto.
We also prototype a collective action tool: <em id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt strikes</em>.
Authors can temporarily withdraw their prompts to protest misuse; the resulting performance drop makes misalignment visible to municipal partners without permanently harming users.
Volunteer-moderation research suggests that such visible levers recognise contributor labour while disciplining governance processes without centralising power <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tabassum et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Risks and mitigations</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dominance capture.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Risk: vocal groups dominate curation.
Mitigation: quotas for inclusion by group and neighbourhood; rotating maintainer seats; publish participation metrics.
Polycentric designs that federate neighbourhood commons into a city layer reduce single-point capture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlisle and Gruby, 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Security &amp; prompt injection.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Risk: adversarial prompts or indirect injection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">OWASP GenAI Security Project, 2023</a>)</cite>.
Mitigation: sandboxed testing; red-teaming; provenance and signed releases; time-to-remediation targets; and hard boundaries between model tools and untrusted content sources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Over-caution.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Risk: excessive neutrality hampers action.
Mitigation: allow decisiveness mode (explicitly labelled) for time-sensitive tasks; track <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and disclose when neutrality thresholds are relaxed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.1 </span>City practice</h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Urban AI should be <em id="S8.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">plural by default</em>—not one prompt, but a commons of prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani, 2025</a>)</cite>.
The Prompt Commons plays three roles in municipal workflows:
(1) evidence of plural values;
(2) reproducible AI configurations;
(3) a procedural space for debate, akin to participatory data stewardship <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ada Lovelace Institute, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ada Lovelace Institute, 2024</a>)</cite>.
This model complements strategic frameworks that place cities at the forefront of responsible AI deployment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu and Liu, 2025</a>)</cite>, aligns with pragmatic adoption realities in local government <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yigitcanlar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and builds on the insight that local governance innovation is essential for trustworthy urban AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allam and Dhunny, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirwan and Fu, 2020</a>)</cite>.
By treating prompts as civic artefacts, we also answer calls to democratise alignment and make visible whose values shape AI behaviour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conitzer et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mushkani et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.2 </span>Limitations and future work</h3>

<div id="S8.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our pilot benchmark is small and focused on Montréal scenes.
We measured satisfaction with a lightweight protocol; future work should adopt mixed methods (surveys, interviews, ethnography).
We used one state-of-practice LLM for the figures; cross-model replication is needed, though our methods are model-agnostic.
While community governance moderates bias at use time, structural ideological leanings in LLMs and alignment artefacts persist <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fulay et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; longitudinal studies should quantify durability of neutrality gains as model families evolve.
Finally, legal evaluation of licensing combinations (CC + OpenRAIL) in municipal procurement warrants further study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDuff et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Contractor et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt governance matters.
By versioning, licensing, and moderating prompts as a civic commons, cities can steer LLM behaviour toward pluralism without sacrificing the ability to act.
In our pilot, single-author prompting reduces neutrality, whereas curated and veto-enabled regimes increase neutral/compromise outcomes, reduce subgroup disparity, and accelerate remediation when harms occur.
We include anonymised datasets and governance materials as supplemental files to support replication and local adaptation.</p>
</div>
</section>
